/id #edb/ref 17592186253329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110587 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b834b64-9679-56c2-b5e9-ee26a54b5e13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cb7114b-10f0-50de-9f4e-3ec3170abc39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251591} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed42e00-97f1-5796-87c4-4a6b558fb4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251618} {:answer/id #uuid "aa369269-44b6-5119-8251-f4a523e27280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252026} {:answer/id #uuid "d05e1c84-fe7d-598c-ad04-62b481bb5bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252395} {:answer/id #uuid "dab066e0-cab3-5c62-a7dc-d26655e66942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ed42e00-97f1-5796-87c4-4a6b558fb4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251618} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a022ae-39e7-51ba-9d4c-e52dc5e65230" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cd41a9a-d0a9-5e6c-a58b-1360b3fd4a17" :question/math-academy-id "q-117328" :question/problem "Which of the following values lies in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $-\\frac{e}{2}$\n- $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n- $50$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, all the given values are in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110588 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29ed49e8-aa20-57b8-b976-39fafdd2efbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250912} {:answer/id #uuid "53671adf-1865-5851-a82c-dae65890db42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251234} {:answer/id #uuid "6e48dfa6-efdd-5ef3-9155-12fa014d2764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251455} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a1e8ce-434e-57eb-9237-3bd53e4aa65a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252128} {:answer/id #uuid "d382ed98-70ad-5611-9eb3-69d637a76575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3a1e8ce-434e-57eb-9237-3bd53e4aa65a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252128} :answer-field/id #uuid "96c901ce-76aa-52f3-923a-e1e306156549" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bfbb710f-9bcf-5482-b898-5f45cf7ceff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-117366" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n- $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110588 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Graph of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86218bc0-259c-5e57-a9f7-9e7223bddb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251677} {:answer/id #uuid "9719f56a-da12-5022-af55-75bf10400eb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251849} {:answer/id #uuid "b57cb08e-ad7e-5089-a3c3-b81c5c3be925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252148} {:answer/id #uuid "dcdc45c3-beea-51df-a813-7a1c4fef0538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252497} {:answer/id #uuid "ef36c6bf-881c-5081-9535-d756f3a835cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef36c6bf-881c-5081-9535-d756f3a835cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252647} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7a12545-b523-55c2-8224-7fdcb91b24af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d01f8dbc-d72d-57bf-bd1d-25c6d02854e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-117371" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{3})$\n- $(0,0)$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{7\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{7\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(0,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,0)$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1616167b-5679-5ff6-b385-3e1b59352c64" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using the Sign of the Slopes and Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04a756b5-7eeb-54aa-9c61-af657974b321" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250589} {:answer/id #uuid "14168a25-afea-5fd7-a63f-be0b85ffec69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250726} {:answer/id #uuid "1db9bcf7-ac2b-5e11-9920-076c94f21a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250806} {:answer/id #uuid "2fb5be26-6c24-584b-bbc8-95a1afca896c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250958} {:answer/id #uuid "4d91da69-8c26-5f36-899d-a55420519710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1db9bcf7-ac2b-5e11-9920-076c94f21a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250806} :answer-field/id #uuid "c72a13c0-2505-5129-9dad-e44544ac13fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f47e52f5-b113-5140-ac0d-d3912c7ac7a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-118025" :question/problem "![](images/50/5077a94fe8b5d5435b5bf50d0ea687f5bc87ae95011e22800ccfe473f8af78dd.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=xy-x$\n- ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}+2xy+x$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/2f/2f7075161363dee2817aeb8aff9e85781a10bd30c926bfff73724992eee699ac.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>-1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(y+1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-x=x(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}+2xy+x=x(y+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256aa671-a669-57d4-b91d-ced24db81e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250876} {:answer/id #uuid "5a277d68-b884-5e7d-b52b-01b0500937b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251295} {:answer/id #uuid "ac672eaa-7a45-596b-aff7-c0a0076f4abb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252053} {:answer/id #uuid "c6240e34-c165-5a5d-b60f-e16d15bc4bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252304} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6959c8-58ee-530d-bb52-f3078500cbf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "256aa671-a669-57d4-b91d-ced24db81e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250876} :answer-field/id #uuid "92e60e35-c089-5683-b48c-03659e05f5af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "260fa3cd-f59d-50c6-a56e-8cfbd29393f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-118755" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}+4$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c9540bd-3579-55ee-9427-762171958cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251189} {:answer/id #uuid "6fee837b-87c6-54d7-a3ac-b451e92ebd95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251465} {:answer/id #uuid "7540a8de-35a1-54d9-8dcb-7408266ac064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251520} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb220c0-023c-5f4c-9950-83aa36861022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251897} {:answer/id #uuid "c40cfe51-cc69-500e-a887-804197c0c982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7540a8de-35a1-54d9-8dcb-7408266ac064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251520} :answer-field/id #uuid "3046fd95-8b83-50f6-b6e3-36de5d1c4279" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "96df78fc-91b5-5b18-90a4-e309d8513bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-118761" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ee83789-4ce5-5132-8655-8c981642cf38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250814} {:answer/id #uuid "3266a5ff-a4d3-5d4c-9c0e-56721f0036d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250979} {:answer/id #uuid "535f340f-8301-565e-bb36-8e145043e9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251233} {:answer/id #uuid "7031f329-ec37-59d1-a199-c5f4140ac2ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251469} {:answer/id #uuid "ee36f28d-0ea6-5254-aeda-afe04cf973e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "535f340f-8301-565e-bb36-8e145043e9b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251233} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0565989-a516-5af3-8a81-7ca93818beb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "412d07fd-1588-561a-99e8-080a2cb49d3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-118783" :question/problem "Which of the following are second-order ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-x$\n- ${y}^{‴}-y=x-{y}^{″}$\n- ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}+x=y$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is of second-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′},$ so this is a first-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253512 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "261fe41f-66fd-591a-9d3e-09ed463bfcc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250881} {:answer/id #uuid "467daa96-9189-5add-9f3c-afcd2ec9af56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251139} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab3bd1f-f80d-5a1d-a7cc-7a11dd1348c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251300} {:answer/id #uuid "9563760a-6dd1-5c49-a006-6fda975970b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251836} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d34e53-59e0-5e62-b65a-58860e6376cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9563760a-6dd1-5c49-a006-6fda975970b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251836} :answer-field/id #uuid "71f2be7e-50e6-547b-bb91-282d9b2201d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4247b2af-dd08-543e-82b1-e9616731f453" :question/math-academy-id "q-118787" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-x=-7y,\\,y(2)=3$\n- ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=0$\n- $x{y}^{″}-6y=14,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-x=-7y$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,$ and ${y}^{″}(0)=0.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $x{y}^{″}-6y=14$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bd7c5a7-2f2a-57f8-8b68-c8b306fe8766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251181} {:answer/id #uuid "7d173ac7-d801-5343-af8c-422d15c088b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251596} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5a853a-1dd7-52c9-9713-a51aa63256be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251721} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e855b2-2a0e-5a63-ade3-f0cbb4a0eccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252539} {:answer/id #uuid "f2bb6e0c-cd51-510d-907f-42ebc72626a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2bb6e0c-cd51-510d-907f-42ebc72626a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252679} :answer-field/id #uuid "da681ee5-4026-5d18-8cbb-0aee21aa73df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "941e9c2c-1b18-5669-bb2b-964e53cd46ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-118788" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(0)=2$\n- $6{y}^{″}+xy={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05cf027e-9ac2-50c5-913b-013f09edb627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250599} {:answer/id #uuid "62c59880-1838-53fa-8ca2-e7dff4e739af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251360} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca9699d-f2d9-5397-b88c-0708539b80b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251904} {:answer/id #uuid "b4098680-b7c4-548d-b62f-2f2a8e17d139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252132} {:answer/id #uuid "d21368d8-4d18-5863-94e3-6b48e3c36458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05cf027e-9ac2-50c5-913b-013f09edb627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250599} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fc3a219-4bfd-592c-aaa9-7e29c36cd584" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c7ca5ad-bcbb-5515-be28-446ac5363a18" :question/math-academy-id "q-118790" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-9x,\\,y(1)=-4$\n- ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y,\\,y(0)=-3$\n- ${y}^{″}+y=3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}=-9x$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=-4.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=-3.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f629dcb-deb6-5bc1-9542-ee7019ade83e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250957} {:answer/id #uuid "80eee9e7-e958-54c7-8dfc-8162423b5453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251639} {:answer/id #uuid "864ac549-5ce9-5d7f-8cc0-9f8450323894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251679} {:answer/id #uuid "86d3cd9d-e46f-567b-ac48-c0f8aa1bff7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251687} {:answer/id #uuid "be15148e-6a47-56a8-a00d-50fc49ad5b04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "864ac549-5ce9-5d7f-8cc0-9f8450323894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251679} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7b864dc-f379-5dd7-b330-e19f03ce5c45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7204e641-7358-50b0-b9c2-d492a160bc98" :question/math-academy-id "q-118791" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x},\\,y(1)=6$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(4)=-3$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253519 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "305095fe-581b-5daa-a454-6a748f03800a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250964} {:answer/id #uuid "4183e9b8-94a7-58ac-9371-c8bc7093f14b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251097} {:answer/id #uuid "a133aeb6-ceff-5917-b87e-69ceb7df5725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251944} {:answer/id #uuid "b21060d2-6b4a-56cb-96fc-3bd5135aca49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252110} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ef4aa2-68f4-562e-a596-0b3f9dd022bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b21060d2-6b4a-56cb-96fc-3bd5135aca49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252110} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8ecce3-7eff-58e1-9e5c-0b494fc4b8f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4fb73e0-2435-50c4-b136-c8ed7ccb224f" :question/math-academy-id "q-118858" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy}=2$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-{e}^{-2y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-{e}^{-2y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2-\\sqrt{xy} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2-\\sqrt{xy}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd93834-0277-5387-aef8-9fb2b2dca832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250662} {:answer/id #uuid "2dfe8bb8-9fe4-58e9-9357-d9810cfd1c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250947} {:answer/id #uuid "5915dc66-227f-5ffa-a6dc-7aa1d5c0003c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251286} {:answer/id #uuid "89de18c4-f123-5cfb-a333-0c7f91f1df60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251713} {:answer/id #uuid "ae98fe96-9458-566e-83e7-67fa2456bcf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89de18c4-f123-5cfb-a333-0c7f91f1df60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251713} :answer-field/id #uuid "89cbcfab-b32e-520a-b3ab-3f4a67487783" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81da2b48-aa38-5eb5-8b7d-567a07263315" :question/math-academy-id "q-118859" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-3{x}^{2}=1$\n- ${y}^{′}+6y={e}^{3y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-3{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+1.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+6y & ={e}^{3y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={e}^{3y}-6y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={e}^{3y}-6y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d03b5cd-402b-5e9e-9a18-d69abf0fecef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250665} {:answer/id #uuid "9afcaa3d-c1de-5df2-a5ef-339b9e0a7f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251885} {:answer/id #uuid "9d395e94-386c-5819-87a8-8bda90430144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251909} {:answer/id #uuid "dcdca02e-8c4d-540e-84e2-e8367000e61a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252498} {:answer/id #uuid "e82d2c6d-1cbb-594d-b6d8-774352ea5c82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d395e94-386c-5819-87a8-8bda90430144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251909} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b734866-2c74-5ecd-a881-13ac9fe9cb09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40dc1226-9a4e-5dff-a14a-c81909c8002d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118868" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{′}+6x{y}^{2}=8$\n- ${y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}=3{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({y}^{2}+2)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+6x{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{8-6x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{8-6x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({y}^{2}+2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cb9eed-b128-5baf-a6ef-6264db0751b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250607} {:answer/id #uuid "166ce4d7-7cb0-5417-9912-96973353838a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250745} {:answer/id #uuid "23b62090-f1be-5651-855a-e9e83de03d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250860} {:answer/id #uuid "332486c7-fce9-5267-827f-8efc6c8ebcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250982} {:answer/id #uuid "d324f459-0823-529b-9901-a4f4bef19942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d324f459-0823-529b-9901-a4f4bef19942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252422} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c4b5e39-6b12-5c74-8707-65e7e8a70568" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1c949f8-b9ab-5207-8749-bf7052ce14f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-118869" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{y}^{2}={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16f980f2-1577-5537-be57-943a8ca18c14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250752} {:answer/id #uuid "5559e3f6-f3c1-51db-b891-67bcbea95dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251252} {:answer/id #uuid "58f44adc-fe68-5d69-a276-6b4ee99d3987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251284} {:answer/id #uuid "80518909-a16e-5896-bc3a-0a5ebcf2ed97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251633} {:answer/id #uuid "dea23c9f-51f5-52ab-a86a-5e2aae041f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58f44adc-fe68-5d69-a276-6b4ee99d3987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251284} :answer-field/id #uuid "a970fe1e-cf80-5fbc-a811-06ed3857f0bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e075483-5dc5-5bbb-be2d-e2d1164eea57" :question/math-academy-id "q-118870" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={e}^{3-y}-2$\n- $y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+y)$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{3-y}-2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7725a5ac-f12f-5a08-9999-bfd41e6696a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251536} {:answer/id #uuid "818adf55-7f8c-555c-9930-b023b5be963f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251644} {:answer/id #uuid "86d89318-8468-53cc-95ab-a4b946c29961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251688} {:answer/id #uuid "8dbca207-78b1-58b0-9bec-e769cd2a732a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251753} {:answer/id #uuid "f97c7e07-12fe-54ca-a7e8-7b7eba160e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "818adf55-7f8c-555c-9930-b023b5be963f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251644} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d67c074-de3d-5624-a3d0-a52f941600ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41da8ad0-e0fc-5f39-8eb7-c4706c551e5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-118976" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcd36221e7ad293f0aac1ab99ece80641cb077efd05adc168a552e97d86100c8.png)\n\nIn parallelogram $ABCD$ shown above, which of the following equalities are true?\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AD}=\\overset{\\to }{CB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}=\\overset{\\to }{DB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\overset{\\to }{FA}$" :question/worked-solution "- Equality I is false. Indeed, $\\overset{\\to }{AD}\\ne \\overset{\\to }{CB}$ since they have the opposite directions.\n\n- Equality II is true. Indeed, $\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}=\\overset{\\to }{DB}.$\n\n- Equality III is false. Indeed, $\\overset{\\to }{AD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\overset{\\to }{AF}\\ne \\overset{\\to }{FA}$ since they have the opposite directions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3aa84ae8-6663-5aa1-844b-89090bcce735" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Slope Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10006a78-2990-5fbf-824c-f21d0f1f27ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250692} {:answer/id #uuid "6adfdb6c-e9a9-5110-96b1-5d2a004665a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251429} {:answer/id #uuid "797abc94-c803-5003-96a4-c3c807c594b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251561} {:answer/id #uuid "c7050d0d-7313-55d2-8d35-7977fc1cbbbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252311} {:answer/id #uuid "c98da25f-3bba-5828-9399-33c0fe2d1fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6adfdb6c-e9a9-5110-96b1-5d2a004665a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251429} :answer-field/id #uuid "26eaa285-2f95-5895-8123-747f0c43b2f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19539888-a72b-5a8c-9bb4-399cabb49fb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-119194" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $x=0,$ the slope field has a positive slope\n- At every point along the line $x=-1,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $x=-2,$ the slope field has a positive slope" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,y)}=2(0)+2=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is positive for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is equal to $2$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(-1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,y)}=2(-1)+2=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(-1,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(-2,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,y)}=2(-2)+2=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(-2,y)$ is negative for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(-2,y)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8d/8d40c405f3f4db66a18e06a5327e86600081a6bc57b502ebed9c94471a0d5857.png)"}]
